# ---------------- architecture ----------------
d("architecture", "Monolith or microservices for a new product with a team of five?", "Start with a modular monolith. One deployable unit is simpler to build, test, debug and run, and with clear module boundaries you can extract a service later if a real need appears (independent scaling, different release cadence, a separate team). Microservices bring network failures, distributed transactions, versioned contracts and heavy operational tooling, which a five-person team usually can't afford early on.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "What problem does a cache solve and what are the risks?", "A cache stores the result of expensive work (a query, a computation, a remote call) so repeated requests are fast. The risks are stale data, inconsistent data across instances, and cache stampedes when a popular key expires and many requests rebuild it at once. Decide how stale is acceptable, set a TTL, invalidate on writes where correctness matters, and add jitter or a lock for hot keys. Measure the hit rate; a cache with a low hit rate only adds complexity.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "When should I use a message queue?", "Use a queue when work doesn't need to finish within the request: sending emails, generating reports, resizing images, calling slow third parties. The web process enqueues a job and returns quickly, and workers process it with retries and rate control. Queues also smooth traffic spikes and decouple components. Remember the cost: at-least-once delivery means jobs can run twice, so make handlers idempotent, and add a dead-letter path for jobs that keep failing.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "What is a layered architecture, and what's the benefit?", "Layers separate responsibilities: presentation (HTTP/UI), application or service logic, domain rules, and data access. Dependencies point one way (top to bottom), so business rules never import web framework code and can be tested without a server or database. The benefit is changeability: you can swap the database or add a CLI without rewriting the rules. The risk is over-layering a small app, where it adds files without adding clarity.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "What does 'stateless' mean for a web service and why does it matter?", "A stateless service keeps no per-user data in its own memory between requests; everything needed arrives with the request or lives in shared storage (database, cache). Then any instance can serve any request, so you can add or remove instances, restart them or deploy without losing sessions, and a load balancer can distribute traffic freely. Keep sessions in a shared store or in signed tokens instead of process memory.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "Explain eventual consistency in plain language.", "After a write, different parts of the system may briefly show different values, but if no new writes occur they all converge to the same value. A social feed that shows your new post to followers a second or two later is an example. It is the price of availability and scale in distributed systems. It is fine for likes and feeds, but not for something like a bank balance, which needs stronger guarantees.", diff="hard")
d("architecture", "How do I decide between SQL and NoSQL for a new project?", "Start from your data and queries. Relational databases suit structured data with relationships, transactions and ad-hoc queries, and are the right default for most applications. Document or key-value stores suit flexible schemas, very high write throughput, or simple access by key, at the cost of weaker joins and cross-record transactions. Many systems use both: a relational database as the system of record plus a cache or search index. Choosing NoSQL only because it \"scales\" is rarely the best reason.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "What is a circuit breaker in a service?", "A circuit breaker wraps calls to a dependency. After enough failures or timeouts it \"opens\" and fails fast for a while instead of waiting on a broken service, then lets a few trial requests through to test recovery. This prevents one slow dependency from tying up all your threads and cascading the outage. Pair it with timeouts, retries with backoff, and a sensible fallback or degraded response.", diff="hard")
d("architecture", "Why shouldn't I let the frontend talk to the database directly?", "The database credentials would have to live in code the user controls, and any user could run arbitrary queries. A backend sits between them to authenticate users, authorise each action, validate input, enforce business rules and limit what is exposed. Some managed platforms do offer direct access, but only with row-level security rules enforced on the database side, which must be designed and tested carefully.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "How do I design for observability?", "Emit three kinds of signals: structured logs (JSON with a request id), metrics (request rate, error rate, latency percentiles, queue depth) and traces that follow a request across services. Propagate a correlation id through every call, log at meaningful levels without secrets or personal data, and alert on symptoms users feel (error rate, latency) rather than only on causes (CPU). Add health endpoints for liveness and readiness.", diff="hard")
d("architecture", "What's the difference between authentication and authorization?", "Authentication answers \"who are you?\": verifying identity with a password, token or key. Authorization answers \"what may you do?\": checking permissions for a specific action on a specific resource. Passing authentication doesn't imply permission. A frequent bug is checking only that a user is logged in and not that the requested record belongs to them (an insecure direct object reference).", diff="easy")
d("architecture", "I want to add background jobs to a small app. Do I need Kafka?", "Almost certainly not. Kafka is a distributed log designed for high-volume event streaming, and it is operationally heavy. For ordinary background jobs, a simple queue backed by your existing database or Redis (or a managed queue) with a worker library is far simpler. Consider Kafka when you need replayable event streams, many independent consumers, or very high throughput.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "What are feature flags and why use them?", "A feature flag is a runtime switch that turns a feature on or off without redeploying. Teams use them to merge unfinished work safely, roll out to a percentage of users, run experiments, and disable a misbehaving feature instantly. The cost is extra code paths and test combinations, so remove flags once a rollout is complete.", diff="medium")
d("architecture", "How should I handle database migrations safely in production?", "Make each migration small and reversible when possible, version them in source control and run them in CI against a copy of the schema. Use the expand/contract pattern for breaking changes: add the new column (nullable), deploy code that writes both, backfill, switch reads, then remove the old column in a later release. Avoid long table locks (add indexes concurrently where supported) and take a backup first.", diff="hard")

# ---------------- testing strategy ----------------
d("testing", "What should I unit test and what should I leave to integration tests?", "Unit-test pure logic and decision-heavy code (calculations, parsing, validation, state transitions), where inputs and outputs are clear and tests are fast. Use integration tests for the seams: your code with a real database, HTTP routes with serialisation and middleware, and external-service adapters (with the service faked at the network edge). A few end-to-end tests cover the most critical user journeys. Many cheap tests at the bottom, fewer slow ones at the top.", diff="medium")
d("testing", "My tests pass locally but fail in CI. Where do I start?", "Look for differences between environments: dependency versions (pin them with a lockfile), environment variables, time zone and locale, file paths and case-sensitivity, and the order of tests. Then flaky causes: shared state between tests, reliance on test order, real network calls, timing (`sleep`) and random data without a seed. Re-run the failing test in isolation and in a fresh clone, read the first failure rather than the last, and make tests independent.", diff="medium")
d("testing", "What is mocking and when does it go wrong?", "A mock replaces a collaborator (a clock, an HTTP client, a payment gateway) with a controllable stand-in so a test is fast and deterministic. It goes wrong when the mock encodes assumptions that are no longer true, so tests pass while the real integration is broken, or when tests assert on how code calls its dependencies rather than on observable results. Mock at system boundaries, prefer fakes for things you own, and keep a few integration tests against the real thing.", diff="medium")
d("testing", "How many assertions should a test have?", "There's no fixed number; a test should check one behaviour. Several assertions are fine when they describe different facets of one outcome (status code and body of one response). If a test needs a paragraph to describe or fails for unrelated reasons, split it. Name tests after the behaviour (`test_rejects_negative_quantity`), so a failure message tells you what broke.", diff="easy")
d("testing", "What does 'test coverage' tell me and what doesn't it?", "Coverage tells you which lines or branches ran during tests. It can reveal untested code, but it can't tell you whether the tests check anything: a test with no assertions still counts. High coverage with weak assertions gives false confidence. Use it to find gaps, aim for meaningful coverage on critical paths, and prefer techniques like mutation testing or code review of the assertions when quality matters.", diff="medium")
d("testing", "Explain test-driven development briefly. Is it worth it?", "TDD means: write a failing test for the next small behaviour, write the simplest code to pass it, then refactor with the tests as a safety net. It yields small, testable designs and a regression suite as a by-product. It is most valuable for logic-heavy code with clear inputs and outputs, and less natural for exploratory UI work. You don't have to follow it dogmatically; writing tests just after the code but in small steps captures much of the benefit.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- Git workflows ----------------
d("git_workflow", "What's a good branching strategy for a small team?", "Trunk-based development with short-lived branches works well: branch from `main`, keep changes small, open a pull request, run CI, review and merge within a day or two, and deploy from `main`. Use feature flags for unfinished work instead of long-running branches. Long-lived branches diverge and produce painful merges. Protect `main` so it requires passing checks and a review.", diff="medium")
d("git_workflow", "I committed a secret to Git and pushed. What do I do?", "First, treat the secret as compromised and rotate it immediately (revoke the old key, issue a new one); removing it from the repo does not undo the exposure. Then remove it from the code and load it from the environment. Optionally rewrite history with a tool such as `git filter-repo` and force-push, but coordinate with collaborators, and remember forks and clones may still hold the old commits. Add secret scanning in CI to catch it next time.", diff="medium")
d("git_workflow", "What makes a good commit message?", "A short imperative summary line (about 50 characters) saying what the commit does, a blank line, then a body explaining why the change was needed and anything non-obvious, wrapped at about 72 columns. Example: `Fix off-by-one in pagination` followed by a sentence describing the symptom and cause. Keep each commit to one logical change so it can be reviewed, reverted or cherry-picked on its own.", diff="easy")
d("git_workflow", "I accidentally committed to main instead of a feature branch. How do I fix it (not pushed yet)?", "Create the branch at your current commit, then move `main` back:\n\n```bash\ngit branch feature/my-change        # keeps the commit on a new branch\ngit reset --hard origin/main         # main goes back to the remote's state\ngit switch feature/my-change\n```\n\nThe `reset --hard` discards uncommitted changes in your working tree, so make sure nothing else is uncommitted. If you have already pushed `main`, don't reset: use `git revert` instead.", diff="medium")
d("git_workflow", "What's the difference between `git fetch`, `git pull` and `git clone`?", "`git clone` copies a whole repository for the first time. `git fetch` downloads new commits and branches from the remote into your remote-tracking branches without touching your working files. `git pull` is `fetch` plus an integration step (merge by default, or rebase if configured) into your current branch. Fetch first when you want to look before you merge.", diff="easy")
d("git_workflow", "My pull request has merge conflicts. What's the cleanest way to resolve it?", "Update your branch with the target branch and resolve conflicts locally:\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main      # or: git merge origin/main\n# edit conflicted files, then:\ngit add <files>\ngit rebase --continue\n```\n\nRebase gives a linear history but rewrites your commits, so you need `git push --force-with-lease` afterwards; use it only on your own branch. Merge is safer if others share the branch. After resolving, rerun the tests, since a clean textual merge can still be a logical conflict.", diff="hard")
d("git_workflow", "What does `git stash` do and when would I use it?", "It saves your uncommitted changes onto a stack and restores a clean working tree, so you can switch branches or pull quickly, then bring the changes back with `git stash pop`. It's handy for context switches, such as an urgent bug fix. Use `git stash push -m \"message\"` to label entries, `git stash list` to see them, and avoid leaving stashes around for weeks, since they are easy to forget.", diff="easy")
d("git_workflow", "How do code reviews work best?", "Keep pull requests small (ideally a few hundred lines) and describe the why, how to test, and anything risky. Reviewers should check correctness, edge cases, tests, readability and security, ask questions rather than issue orders, and separate must-fix from nice-to-have comments. Automate the mechanical things (formatting, linting, tests) so humans can focus on design and logic. Respond to feedback promptly in both directions.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- debugging dialogs (multi-turn) ----------------
d("debug_dialog", "My React app shows a blank page after I deployed it to a subfolder (example.com/app/). It worked locally.", "That pattern usually means asset paths are wrong. Open the browser's developer tools: in the Network tab, are the JavaScript files returning 404 (probably requesting `/assets/...` from the domain root)? And does the Console show errors?", "yes, the js files 404. they're requested from /assets/index.js", "Then your build assumes it's served from `/`. Tell the bundler the base path. With Vite, set `base: '/app/'` in `vite.config.js` and rebuild; with Create React App, set `homepage` in `package.json`. If you use client-side routing, also configure the router's basename (for React Router: `<BrowserRouter basename=\"/app\">`), and make the server fall back to `index.html` for unknown paths under `/app/` so refreshing a deep link doesn't 404.", "now the page loads but refreshing /app/settings gives 404", "That's the server-side fallback. Static hosts only serve files that exist, so `/app/settings` isn't found. Configure a rewrite to `index.html`: in nginx, `location /app/ { try_files $uri /app/index.html; }`; on many static hosts there's a setting such as 'single-page app' or a rewrite rule file. The router then reads the URL and renders the right screen.", diff="medium")
d("debug_dialog", "My API is slow, around 3 seconds per request. Where do I start?", "Measure before guessing. Break the request time into parts: network, application code, database, external calls. Do you have logging, a profiler or APM, or can you at least time the main steps? And is it slow for every endpoint or just one?", "just the /orders list endpoint. it loads orders and their items", "That sounds like an N+1 query problem: one query for the orders, then one query per order for its items. Enable query logging and count the SQL statements per request. If you see hundreds, load the items in bulk with a join or an `IN (...)` query (with an ORM, use eager loading such as `selectinload` or `joinedload`). Also check that the foreign key column `order_id` is indexed.", "yes 240 queries. I added eager loading and it's 4 queries now, but still 800ms", "Good progress. Now look at those four: run `EXPLAIN` (or `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`) on each and look for full table scans or large sorts. Check whether you return more data than the page needs (select only the needed columns, paginate). If the numbers are still large, a covering index for the filter and sort columns, or caching the result for a short time, is the next step.", diff="hard")
d("debug_dialog", "Python: my script prints 'ModuleNotFoundError: No module named requests' even though I installed it.", "That usually means you installed it into a different Python than the one running the script. Run `python -m pip show requests` and `python -c \"import sys; print(sys.executable)\"` with the same interpreter. Are you using a virtual environment?", "I have two pythons. pip installs to 3.9 but I run python3.12", "Install with the interpreter you run, so they always match: `python3.12 -m pip install requests`. Better, create a virtual environment for the project (`python3.12 -m venv .venv`, activate it, then `pip install -r requirements.txt`) so dependencies stay isolated and `python` and `pip` refer to the same environment.", diff="easy")
d("debug_dialog", "git push says 'rejected - non-fast-forward'. What now?", "It means the remote branch has commits you don't have locally, so Git won't overwrite them. Run `git fetch` and look at `git log --oneline HEAD..origin/<branch>` to see what's there. Is this a shared branch or just yours?", "shared with a teammate. they pushed this morning", "Then integrate their work first: `git pull --rebase` (or `git pull` for a merge) to put your commits on top of theirs, resolve any conflicts, run the tests, and push again. Don't use `--force` on a shared branch, since it would delete your teammate's commits from the remote.", diff="medium")
d("debug_dialog", "My Docker container exits immediately after starting.", "Check why: `docker logs <container>` shows its output, and `docker ps -a` shows the exit code. A container stops when its main process ends or crashes. What does the Dockerfile run (`CMD` or `ENTRYPOINT`)?", "CMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"] and the log says 'No such file or directory: app.py'", "The file isn't where the container expects. Make sure it is copied in and that the working directory is right:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY . /app\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n```\n\nAlso check `.dockerignore` isn't excluding it, and that the build context contains `app.py`. You can look inside with `docker run --rm -it <image> sh` and `ls`.", diff="medium")
d("debug_dialog", "Why is `fetch('/api/data')` returning HTML instead of JSON in my SPA?", "A request for `/api/data` is receiving your app's `index.html`, which means the request isn't reaching the API. Common causes: the dev server has no proxy configured for `/api`, the production server's SPA fallback rule catches `/api` too, or the API runs on another port or host. Check the Network tab for the response and which server answered. Which setup are you using in development?", "Vite dev server, API on port 8000", "Add a proxy to `vite.config.js` so `/api` goes to the backend:\n\n```javascript\nexport default {\n  server: { proxy: { '/api': 'http://localhost:8000' } },\n};\n```\n\nRestart the dev server. In production, make your reverse proxy route `/api` to the backend before the fallback to `index.html`.", diff="medium")
d("debug_dialog", "TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map') in my React component. I'm doing `data.items.map(...)`.", "`data.items` is `undefined` at the moment of the render. Typically the data hasn't loaded yet (initial state is `null` or `{}`), or the API response has a different shape than you expect. Do you initialise `data`, and what does the response look like?", "useState(null) and then I fetch. the response is {\"results\": [...]}", "Two bugs: the response key is `results`, not `items`, and the first render happens before the fetch completes. Guard the render and use the right field:\n\n```jsx\nif (!data) return <p>Loading...</p>;\nreturn data.results.map(r => <Row key={r.id} row={r} />);\n```\n\nIf the shape is uncertain, log `console.log(data)` once, or validate the response before putting it in state.", diff="easy")
d("debug_dialog", "My cron job runs fine manually but not from cron.", "The usual cause is the environment: cron runs with a minimal `PATH`, no shell profile, a different working directory and no virtualenv. Use absolute paths for the interpreter and script, set the working directory, and capture output so you can see the error: `* * * * * cd /srv/app && /srv/app/.venv/bin/python job.py >> /var/log/job.log 2>&1`. Does anything in your script depend on environment variables?", "yes, it needs DATABASE_URL", "Cron doesn't load your shell's variables. Define it in the crontab (`DATABASE_URL=...` at the top), source an env file in the command (`set -a; . /etc/app.env; set +a;`), or have the script read the settings file itself. Keep the secret file readable only by the job's user.", diff="medium")
d("debug_dialog", "I changed my CSS but the browser still shows the old styles.", "That's almost always caching. Try a hard refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R) or open the dev tools Network tab with 'Disable cache' ticked. If it persists, check that the file you edited is the one actually loaded (view the source of the stylesheet in the Sources tab), that the build step ran, and whether a service worker is serving an old copy. Is this local or a deployed site?", "deployed behind a CDN", "Then the CDN is serving a cached copy. Best practice is to fingerprint filenames at build time (`app.3f2a1c.css`) so every change gets a new URL and you can cache forever; HTML should have a short cache time or revalidate. As a quick fix, purge the file in the CDN.", diff="medium")
d("debug_dialog", "Why does `npm install` give me peer dependency errors?", "A package you're installing declares that it needs a specific version range of another package (a peer dependency), and your project has a version outside that range. Read the message: it names the package, the required range and what you have. Which packages does it mention?", "react 19 but a library wants react ^18", "Check whether the library has a newer release that supports React 19; upgrading it is the clean fix. If not, you can stay on React 18 until it does. `--legacy-peer-deps` or `--force` will bypass the check, but that can leave you with a library that breaks at runtime, so use it only knowingly and test the app.", diff="medium")

# ---------------- executed Node snippets ----------------
RUNS = [
 ("node_exec", "medium", "What's the output of this Node.js code, and why?", 'const results = [];\nfor (const delay of [30, 10, 20]) {\n  setTimeout(() => results.push(delay), delay);\n}\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(results.join(",")), 50);',
  "It prints `{out}`. Each `setTimeout` is scheduled with its own delay, so the callbacks fire in order of delay (10, 20, 30), not in the order they were created. The last timer (50 ms) fires after all of them and prints the collected values."),
 ("node_exec", "medium", "Does this async code run the two requests in parallel or one after another?", 'const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));\n(async () => {\n  const t0 = Date.now();\n  await wait(60);\n  await wait(60);\n  const seq = Date.now() - t0 >= 110;\n  const t1 = Date.now();\n  await Promise.all([wait(60), wait(60)]);\n  const par = Date.now() - t1 < 110;\n  console.log("sequential takes about the sum:", seq, "| parallel takes about the max:", par);\n})();',
  "Awaiting one after the other runs them sequentially, so the time is the sum of both. `Promise.all` starts both before waiting, so the time is the longer one. The check prints:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nUse `Promise.all` for independent operations, but keep sequential awaits when the second depends on the first."),
 ("node_exec", "easy", "How do I parse a query string safely in Node?", 'const url = new URL("https://example.com/search?q=node+js&page=2&tag=a&tag=b");\nconsole.log(url.searchParams.get("q"));\nconsole.log(Number(url.searchParams.get("page")));\nconsole.log(url.searchParams.getAll("tag"));',
  "Use the built-in `URL` and `URLSearchParams` instead of splitting strings by hand; they handle decoding (`+` becomes a space) and repeated keys. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nRemember that every value is a string, so convert and validate numbers, and use `getAll` for keys that can repeat."),
 ("node_exec", "medium", "How do I read JSON from a string and handle bad input?", 'function parse(text) {\n  try {\n    return { ok: true, value: JSON.parse(text) };\n  } catch (e) {\n    return { ok: false, error: e.name };\n  }\n}\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(parse(\'{"a": 1}\')));\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(parse("{a: 1}")));',
  "`JSON.parse` throws on invalid input, so wrap it and return a result instead of letting the exception escape a request handler. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nParsing only proves the text is JSON; you still need to validate that the value has the fields and types you expect."),
 ("node_exec", "medium", "How do I make a simple in-memory cache with expiry in Node?", 'class TtlCache {\n  constructor(ttlMs) { this.ttl = ttlMs; this.map = new Map(); }\n  set(k, v) { this.map.set(k, { v, exp: Date.now() + this.ttl }); }\n  get(k) {\n    const e = this.map.get(k);\n    if (!e) return undefined;\n    if (e.exp < Date.now()) { this.map.delete(k); return undefined; }\n    return e.v;\n  }\n}\nconst c = new TtlCache(30);\nc.set("a", 1);\nconsole.log(c.get("a"));\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(c.get("a")), 60);',
  "Store each value with an expiry timestamp and check it on read, deleting expired entries lazily. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nThis is fine for one process. It has no size limit (add an eviction policy such as LRU for real use), and with several server instances each has its own copy, so use a shared cache like Redis when consistency matters."),
 ("node_exec", "hard", "Why does this loop block the server even though I used async/await?", 'let ticks = 0;\nconst timer = setInterval(() => ticks++, 10);\n(async () => {\n  const end = Date.now() + 100;\n  while (Date.now() < end) {}          // synchronous busy loop inside an async function\n  console.log("ticks during busy loop:", ticks);\n  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 55));\n  console.log("ticks after yielding:", ticks > 0);\n  clearInterval(timer);\n})();',
  "`async` doesn't make code run in the background; it only lets `await` yield. A synchronous loop runs to completion on the single thread, so the 10 ms timer cannot fire while it spins. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nOnce the code awaits, the event loop gets control back and the timer runs. For CPU-heavy work use a worker thread or a separate process."),
 ("node_exec", "medium", "How can I create a unique, safe filename for an upload in Node?", 'const crypto = require("node:crypto");\nconst path = require("node:path");\nfunction storedName(original) {\n  const ext = path.extname(original).toLowerCase();\n  const allowed = new Set([".png", ".jpg", ".pdf"]);\n  if (!allowed.has(ext)) throw new Error("type not allowed");\n  return crypto.randomUUID() + ext;\n}\nconst n = storedName("../../My Photo.PNG");\nconsole.log(/^[0-9a-f-]{36}\\.png$/.test(n));\ntry { storedName("run.exe"); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); }',
  "Ignore the client's file name for storage: generate a random name and keep only an allow-listed extension. That removes path traversal (`../../`), odd characters and collisions. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nKeep the original name in the database if you want to show it to the user, and check the file's content type as well."),
]
for i, (sub, level, ask, code, ans) in enumerate(RUNS):
    ok, out = run_js(code)
    if not ok:
        print("DROP", ask, out[:100])
        continue
    out = "\n".join(l.rstrip() for l in out.strip().splitlines())
    user = f"{ask}\n\n```javascript\n{code}\n```"
    B.d(sub, user, ans.replace("{out}", out), diff=level, src="verified")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    print(len(B.items))
